{"id":"exempt-appointments-reported","title":"Exempt appointments as Human Resources reports them","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"How many exempt appointments does the Department of Human Resources report by Charter category and department, how do they compare with the payroll, and how many does it report as past their time limit?","finding":"As of 30 June 2026 Human Resources reported 1,847 appointments in Charter category 16 (temporary and seasonal), 83 in category 17 (substitutes) and 2,309 in category 18 (special projects), 4,239 in all, outside the transportation agency. The Charter limits are half-time in a fiscal year (1,040 hours) for category 16, two years for 17 and three years for 18. Human Resources listed 5 appointments past the limit in its report of 15 September 2025, 6 appointments past the limit in its report of 5 October 2026, and wrote in 2 earlier reports that none had exceeded it. The payroll, on a different footing, shows 740 people outside the transportation agency paid as temporary exempt for more than 1,040 hours a year in the same class and department for four or more fiscal years running. The share of employees in the capped categories (1, 2 and 4 to 12) was reported as 1.56% on 30 June 2026.","how":"Figures printed in the Department of Human Resources’ reports to the Civil Service Commission, August 2023 to October 2026: appointments by category, approved position requests by department and category (the report heard on 5 October 2026), and the listing of category 16 appointments by department (heard on 19 August 2024). Expired appointments are counted from the position lines of the listings attached to those reports; names are not read. The payroll columns are for fiscal 2026: people paid on a temporary exempt appointment, and full-time equivalents on permanent exempt ones. Ranked by approved requests. The reports leave out the transportation agency, the courts, the college district and the school district.","limits":"The two sources count different things. Human Resources counts appointments by Charter category on one day; the payroll records a broad type for everyone paid during a year and no category. A person paid as temporary exempt for years may hold a category with no time limit, may have moved between positions, or may be in a class such as per-diem nursing; so the payroll figure is a question to put to Human Resources, not a count of breaches. Categories 1, 2 and 4 to 12 are reported only as one citywide percentage, so exempt managers and deputies cannot be counted by department from these reports. Department listings add up to 96 to 100% of the printed totals, and reports before August 2023 are not served.","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"cat16","t":"Category 16 appointments listed","type":"int"},{"k":"payrollTemp","t":"Payroll: people paid as temporary exempt, fiscal 2026","type":"int"},{"k":"requests16","t":"Approved requests, category 16","type":"int"},{"k":"requests17","t":"Category 17","type":"int"},{"k":"requests18","t":"Category 18","type":"int"},{"k":"headCount","t":"Head count allowed by those requests","type":"int"},{"k":"payrollPermExempt","t":"Payroll: permanent exempt full-time equivalents","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"department":"Public Health","cat16":186,"payrollTemp":2224,"requests16":146,"requests17":66,"requests18":153,"headCount":370,"payrollPermExempt":217},{"department":"Public Works","cat16":26,"payrollTemp":248,"requests16":5,"requests17":0,"requests18":217,"headCount":222,"payrollPermExempt":70},{"department":"Public Utilities Commission","cat16":63,"payrollTemp":398,"requests16":2,"requests17":10,"requests18":109,"headCount":216,"payrollPermExempt":107},{"department":"Police","cat16":16,"payrollTemp":379,"requests16":1,"requests17":1,"requests18":71,"headCount":73,"payrollPermExempt":135},{"department":"Recreation and Park","cat16":943,"payrollTemp":1214,"requests16":12,"requests17":0,"requests18":56,"headCount":390,"payrollPermExemp